    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_6f53cefc06b994f1611a79c636598aa86623bd49c8c106ed7eaddaf971eddb80",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Expanding the zeta integral first isolates the multiplicative equality sector as the sole source of the basic w=1 pole, while oscillation continues the other sectors. The residue's Euler product then separates pairwise zeta poles from the regular arithmetic factor. Quasi-functional equations identify the phase-zero stabilizer that reflects this base pole; conditional completeness of that reflected divisor permits model-orbit subtraction, and the orbit-cancellation lemma reduces its leading normalized coefficient to the arithmetic central value times the universal combinatorial factor that the Tauberian theorem converts into moment growth.",
