    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_725f978d4b1a43e4b5047d1b3a4ccaeac434c3b601ffb2b2febbfdf055aa71ff",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Smoothing and sharp cutoff demand materially different analytic resources. The triangular kernel contributes an extra denominator that both defines the weight and gives enough vertical decay for a direct contour shift. Without that smoothing, signed cubic coefficients require truncated Perron and short-interval mass control obtained from second- and fourth-moment inputs; the shifted rectangle creates a ledger of vertical, horizontal, truncation, and possible reflected-pole terms. For small sieve moduli a functional-equation reflection exposes oscillatory gamma ratios whose phase curvature yields a half-power saving, while large moduli use a near-one contour; the published unweighted exponent is then obtained by balancing the surviving errors across the two modulus regimes.",
