    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_e2ea47999ae9bd1a343495dda8106377d31f293b70b29f2737fc3cc6c66f7705",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The zeta and quadratic-family constructions use different base diagonals and reflection arrangements, but the same two-part leading-coefficient mechanism. In the zeta case a multiplicative equality residue yields the regular factor a_2m and a balanced opposite-sign reflection orbit; in the quadratic family reciprocity selects square products, whose residue yields a_m and whose coordinate-reflection orbit has 2^m members. In both cases antisymmetric divisibility makes the orbit sum removable and homogeneity discards positive-degree Taylor pieces only in the leading normalized limit, leaving the arithmetic central value multiplied by a universal combinatorial coefficient.",
